    { "@type": "Question", "name": "When is the best time to prune trees in Ohio?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Late winter — February through early March — is the best general pruning window for most NE Ohio species. Trees are dormant, structural problems are visible without leaves, and wounds seal cleanly before spring pathogen pressure begins. The critical exception: NO oak pruning April 1 through July 31, when nitidulid beetles vector oak wilt in Ohio. Roubic refuses oak work during that window regardless of customer urgency." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Why won't you prune my oak in May?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Oak wilt is a fungal vascular disease vectored by sap-feeding nitidulid beetles in Ohio, and the high-risk window runs roughly April 1 through July 31. Pruning during that window creates fresh wounds the beetles are attracted to. A single infected pruning cut can introduce oak wilt that kills the tree within two seasons and infects adjacent oaks via root grafts. We won't make that mistake on your property — the disease is too severe and too transmissible." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "How often should I trim my trees?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "For mature healthy trees, every 2–3 years is typical for routine maintenance. Estate properties with multi-tree maintenance contracts run on annual or semi-annual rotations. Fruit trees need annual pruning for yield. Damaged or diseased trees may need more frequent work. Don't remove more than 25% of live canopy in a single season — over-pruning starves the tree." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "What's the difference between trimming and pruning?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "In casual usage they're often interchangeable, but in arboricultural practice 'pruning' refers to the broader category of selective branch removal — for structure, health, safety, or aesthetics — and 'trimming' is more often used for routine maintenance and shape work. Both follow the same ANSI A300 standards. We use both terms, but the technique is the technique." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Do you follow ANSI A300 standards?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. All of our pruning work follows ANSI A300 — the industry-recognized specification for tree care, published by the Tree Care Industry Association. That means three-cut technique on any limb over two inches, cuts placed just outside the branch collar, no flush cuts, no stub cuts, and never more than 25% of live canopy removed in a single season." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Is topping a tree ever appropriate?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No. Topping permanently destroys tree structure, creates massive decay entry points, and triggers weakly-attached water sprouts that fail catastrophically 5–10 years later. If a tree is genuinely too tall for its location, the answer is crown reduction by a qualified pruner using ANSI A300 standards, not topping. Topping was standard practice 30 years ago and is now considered malpractice in the industry." } },
